          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Ub2e1a6NFWzQCqyMwUxWYS",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022tech/sqlite-wal-mode.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Technology\\ntitle: SQLite WAL Mode\\ndescription: Write-Ahead Logging mode in SQLite enables concurrent readers alongside a single writer by trading minor read staleness for better write concurrency.\\ntimestamp: 2025-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - tech/SQLite\\n  - concept/Concurrency\\n  - concept/Database-Mode-Selection\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single sourced claim about WAL\\u0027s trade-off and concurrency model; no contradicting sources found.\\u0022\\nresource: https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nSQLite\\u0027s **Write-Ahead Log (WAL)** mode is an alternative to the default rollback-journal mechanism for managing transactions and concurrent access.\\n\\n## Mechanism and Trade-off\\n\\nIn WAL mode, changes are appended to a separate log file rather than written directly to the database file. SQLite then periodically **checkpoints** the log entries back into the main database, consolidating the changes. This design allows multiple readers to access the database concurrently while a single writer appends to the log \\u2014 a capability not available in the default rollback-journal mode.\\n\\nThe cost is **read staleness tolerance**: readers may see committed data that is slightly older than the absolute latest writes, depending on checkpoint timing. This trade-off is often acceptable or even desirable for applications with moderate concurrency requirements.\\n\\n## Decision Context: Single-Writer Embedded Services\\n\\nFor embedded services with a **single writer** (e.g., an in-process service managing its own SQLite instance):\\n\\n- **Rollback-journal mode** (default): Simple, no read staleness; readers block on writes and writers block on readers. Suitable when concurrency is not a primary concern.\\n- **WAL mode**: Enables concurrent reads while a single writer appends. The read staleness window is typically small and bounded by checkpoint frequency. Recommended when read-heavy workloads coexist with writes.\\n\\nThe choice depends on the service\\u0027s read-to-write ratio and tolerance for eventual consistency within the same process or immediate replication context.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Inferred from SQLite WAL design (single writer \\u002B concurrent readers model).\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
